(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-brain-ans-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Brain and the Autonomic Nervous System — Anatomy Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"What are arachnoid granulations, and what is their function?","choices":["Blood vessels that specifically supply the choroid plexus with arterial blood flow","Projections of the arachnoid mater through which CSF flows to enter blood circulation","Clusters of autonomic nuclei located deep within the medulla oblongata itself","Folds of the dura mater that separate the two cerebral hemispheres from each other"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arachnoid granulations are projections of the arachnoid mater through which CSF flows to enter into blood circulation. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What underlies the arachnoid layer, and what is its function?","choices":["The choroid plexus, which actively produces cerebrospinal fluid","Weblike collagenous material that helps anchor cerebral blood vessels","The dural sinus, which primarily drains venous blood away from the brain","A rigid layer of bone that lies directly beneath the overlying skull"],"answer":1,"exp":"Underlying the arachnoid layer is weblike material consisting of collagenous fibers that help anchor cerebral blood vessels. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What does the pia mater do, and what does it follow?","choices":["Lines the inside of the skull bones only, forming the outer periosteal layer of the dura mater and playing no other role","Forms a loose, weblike covering that sits separate from the brain surface, bridging directly over the sulci rather than dipping into them","Attached to the surface of the brain, cerebellum, and spinal cord; follows the sulci and gyri of the brain and helps anchor larger cerebral blood vessels","Produces cerebrospinal fluid within the ventricles via the choroid plexus tissue, and never directly touches the brain's surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pia mater is attached to the surface of the brain, cerebellum, and spinal cord, following the sulci and gyri of the brain, and helps anchor the larger blood vessels of the cerebrum. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What are the functions of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), and where is it produced?","choices":["Regulates body temperature and systemic blood pressure; produced entirely by the dura mater's inner layer","Transports oxygen and glucose directly to neurons; produced entirely by the arachnoid granulations themselves","Provides protection, support, and nutrients to the CNS and transports waste away; produced by ependymal cells of the choroid plexus","Provides only structural support, with no nutrient or waste-clearance role at all; produced entirely by the pia mater"],"answer":2,"exp":"CSF provides protection, support, and nutrients to the CNS and transports waste away from the CNS; it is produced by the ependymal cells of the choroid plexus. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is the correct order of CSF circulation, starting from the choroid plexus of the lateral ventricles?","choices":["Lateral ventricles → intraventricular foramen → 3rd ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → 4th ventricle → subarachnoid space → arachnoid granulations/villi","Subarachnoid space → 4th ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → 3rd ventricle → lateral ventricles → central canal, in reverse","4th ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → 3rd ventricle → intraventricular foramen → lateral ventricles → central canal only","Lateral ventricles → subarachnoid space directly, entirely bypassing the 3rd and 4th ventricles and cerebral aqueduct"],"answer":0,"exp":"CSF circulates from the choroid plexus of the lateral ventricles, through the intraventricular foramen, into the 3rd ventricle, through the cerebral aqueduct, into the 4th ventricle, then through the lateral and median apertures and central canal into the subarachnoid space, eventually entering circulation via the arachnoid granulations and villi. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What structural feature of brain blood vessels creates the blood-brain barrier?","choices":["A thick layer of dense bone directly surrounding the vessels","A near-total absence of capillaries within brain tissue","Tight endothelial cell junctions lining the blood vessels","A thick layer of adipose tissue surrounding each vessel"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lining of brain blood vessels consists of tight endothelial cell junctions, which create the blood-brain barrier. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What types of materials can pass through the blood-brain barrier, and how?","choices":["Both lipid- and water-soluble materials pass freely through the barrier without any restriction at all","No materials of any kind, whether lipid- or water-soluble, can ever pass through the blood-brain barrier","Only water-soluble materials can pass, and they do so freely without any transport mechanisms needed","Lipid-soluble materials pass through directly; water-soluble materials pass only via specific transport mechanisms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Due to the tight endothelial junctions, only lipid-soluble materials can pass through the blood-brain barrier directly, while water-soluble material can only pass via specific transport mechanisms. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What does \"autonomic\" mean, and what functions does the ANS regulate?","choices":["\"Automatic,\" but its entire regulatory role is limited strictly to controlling heart rate and rhythm alone","\"Automatic,\" outside conscious awareness; regulates body temperature, digestion, reproductive functions, breathing, and heart beat","\"Voluntary,\" though still technically outside conscious awareness; regulates only digestive processes exclusively","\"Automatic,\" yet fully under conscious voluntary control; regulates only skeletal muscle movement and posture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Autonomic means \"automatic\" — outside of our conscious awareness — and the ANS regulates functions like body temperature, digestion, reproductive functions, breathing, and heart beat. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How do autonomic efferent neurons differ from somatic efferent nerves in terms of ganglia?","choices":["Neither type of efferent neuron involves ganglia at all; both somatic and autonomic fibers travel directly from the CNS to their target tissue without any intervening synapse whatsoever","Autonomic efferent neurons innervate skeletal muscle directly with no ganglia involved; somatic efferent nerves innervate visceral organs and synapse in ganglia located outside the CNS","Autonomic efferent neurons innervate visceral organs and have ganglionic neurons that synapse in ganglia outside the CNS; somatic efferent nerves innervate skeletal muscle and do not involve ganglia","Both types of efferent neurons synapse identically in ganglia located just outside the spinal cord, with no meaningful functional distinction existing between the two systems at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Autonomic efferent neurons innervate visceral organs and have ganglionic neurons that synapse in ganglia outside the CNS, while somatic efferent nerves innervate skeletal muscle and do not involve ganglia. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What are the three divisions of the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["Sympathetic, somatic, and parasympathetic, with no enteric division","Craniosacral, thoracolumbar, and somatic, with enteric excluded entirely","Only two total divisions exist: sympathetic and parasympathetic alone","Sympathetic (thoracolumbar), parasympathetic (craniosacral), and enteric"],"answer":3,"exp":"The three divisions of the autonomic nervous system are the sympathetic (thoracolumbar division), parasympathetic (craniosacral division), and enteric nervous system. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is \"dual innervation,\" and which organs have it?","choices":["Only the heart and lungs have dual innervation; every other organ has single innervation","Most vital organs are innervated by both the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems","Dual innervation refers specifically to innervation from two separate spinal cord segments","Dual innervation means innervation by both somatic and autonomic fibers exclusively together"],"answer":1,"exp":"Dual innervation means most vital organs are innervated by both the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"When is the sympathetic division most active, and what are its general effects?","choices":["Most active during rest and sleep; causes relaxation, promotes digestion, and gradual, ongoing energy storage processes throughout","Most active constantly and equally at all times, showing absolutely no meaningful variation based on physiologic state or context","Most active during stress, exertion, or emergency; increases cardiovascular/respiratory activity, muscle tone, alertness, and energy mobilization","Most active during sleep only; produces no measurable cardiovascular, respiratory, or muscular effect of any kind whatsoever"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sympathetic division is most active during stress, exertion, or emergency, producing increased cardiovascular/respiratory activity, increased muscle tone and alertness, and increased mobilization of energy reserves. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"When is the parasympathetic division most active, and what are its general effects?","choices":["Most active constantly and continuously, functioning nearly identically to the sympathetic division","Most active only during vigorous exercise or periods of intense physical exertion","Most active during stress or emergency; increases alertness, heart rate, and energy mobilization","Most active during rest; causes relaxation, pupillary constriction, digestion, and energy absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"The parasympathetic division is most active during rest, producing relaxation, pupillary constriction, digestion, and energy absorption. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is the enteric nervous system, sometimes called \"the brain of the gut\"?","choices":["A subdivision of the sympathetic nervous system that has no independent function whatsoever and cannot act at all without constant, direct input from the spinal cord itself","Neurons located in the walls of the digestive tract that can initiate many visceral reflexes independent of the brain and spinal cord, while receiving input from the ANS","A division of the autonomic nervous system that only innervates the heart and lungs directly, never the digestive tract itself, despite what its common nickname suggests","A division of the central nervous system located entirely within the lower spinal cord segments, controlling only basic spinal reflex arcs and nothing else at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"The enteric nervous system consists of neurons located in the walls of the digestive tract; many visceral reflexes are initiated by the ENS independent of the brain and spinal cord, though it receives input from the ANS. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Where are the sympathetic chains located, and what is the difference between preganglionic and postganglionic fibers?","choices":["Both fiber types are entirely identical in length, myelination status, and the specific neurotransmitter each one releases at its synaptic terminal, with absolutely no meaningful difference recognized between them","Parallel chains on either side of the spinal cord in the thoracolumbar region; preganglionic fibers are short/myelinated (release acetylcholine), postganglionic fibers are long/unmyelinated (mostly release norepinephrine)","Located within the cranial vault only, not the spinal cord; preganglionic fibers are long and unmyelinated, postganglionic fibers are short and myelinated, and both release acetylcholine exclusively at every synapse","Located only within the cranial vault, never alongside the spinal cord itself; both preganglionic and postganglionic fiber types release only acetylcholine at every single synapse they form"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sympathetic chains are parallel chains on either side of the spinal cord in the thoracolumbar region; preganglionic fibers are short and myelinated, extending from the spinal cord to the sympathetic chain (releasing acetylcholine), while postganglionic fibers are long and unmyelinated, extending to effector organs (mostly releasing norepinephrine). — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What do abdominopelvic splanchnic nerves carry, and what are the four named splanchnic nerves?","choices":["Carry only presynaptic fibers directly to the heart and lungs; named as the cardiac, pulmonary, gastric, and hepatic splanchnic nerves","Carry only general sensory fibers originating from the skin of the abdomen and pelvis, never any motor or autonomic fibers at all","Carry postsynaptic fibers to abdominal and pelvic viscera; the greater, lesser, least, and lumbar splanchnic nerves","Carry parasympathetic fibers exclusively, and are instead named the vagal, pelvic, and sacral splanchnic nerve groups"],"answer":2,"exp":"Abdominopelvic splanchnic nerves carry postsynaptic fibers to abdominal and pelvic viscera, and include the greater splanchnic nerve, lesser splanchnic nerve, least splanchnic nerve, and lumbar splanchnic nerve. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What do cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves carry, and where distal to the paravertebral ganglia do all sympathetic nerves become splanchnic nerves?","choices":["Carry postsynaptic fibers to thoracic viscera; distal to the paravertebral ganglia, all sympathetic nerves become splanchnic nerves","Carry only presynaptic fibers to thoracic viscera, and are said to never carry any postsynaptic fibers whatsoever","Carry postsynaptic fibers exclusively to the lower limbs, entirely bypassing the thoracic visceral organs altogether","Are entirely unrelated to the paravertebral ganglia, arising instead directly from the dorsal spinal nerve roots themselves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cardiopulmonary splanchnic nerves carry postsynaptic fibers to thoracic viscera, and distal to the paravertebral ganglia, all sympathetic nerves become splanchnic nerves. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which cranial nerves and spinal segments are involved in the parasympathetic division?","choices":["Thoracolumbar spinal segments T1 through L2 only, with absolutely no cranial nerve involvement at all","General visceral efferent fibers of CN III, VII, IX, and X, plus the bulbar portion of CN XI, and sacral segments S2-S4","General somatic afferent fibers of CN V, VI, VIII, and XII, along with cervical spinal segments C1 through C4","Only cranial nerves III and IV, entirely without any spinal cord segment involvement whatsoever"],"answer":1,"exp":"The parasympathetic division involves the general visceral efferent fibers of CN III, VII, IX, and X, and the bulbar portion of CN XI, along with sacral segments S2 to S4. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What type of neurotransmitter do all parasympathetic neurons release, and what receptor do they stimulate at ganglionic synapses?","choices":["Acetylcholine; stimulates only muscarinic receptors at the ganglion, which is always inhibitory","Norepinephrine (adrenergic); stimulates alpha and beta receptors, which is always inhibitory in effect","Dopamine; stimulates nicotinic receptors on ganglionic neurons, which is always an inhibitory effect","Acetylcholine (cholinergic); stimulates nicotinic receptors on ganglionic neurons, which is always excitatory"],"answer":3,"exp":"All parasympathetic neurons are cholinergic, releasing acetylcholine (ACh); at ganglionic synapses, ACh stimulates nicotinic receptors on ganglionic neurons, and this effect is always excitatory. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"At the neuroeffector junction, what receptor does ACh stimulate, and can the effect be excitatory or inhibitory?","choices":["Nicotinic receptors; the effect is always purely excitatory in nature","Muscarinic receptors; the effect can be either excitatory or inhibitory","Nicotinic receptors; the effect is always purely inhibitory in nature","Adrenergic receptors; the effect is always purely excitatory in nature"],"answer":1,"exp":"At neuroeffector junctions, ACh stimulates muscarinic receptors, and the effect can be either excitatory or inhibitory depending on the target tissue. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve synapses in the ciliary ganglion, and what does it innervate?","choices":["CN VII; innervates the parotid gland","CN III; innervates the iris/pupillary ciliary muscles","CN IX; innervates glands of the eyes, nose, and mouth","CN X; innervates the terminal ganglia of the heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"CN III synapses in the ciliary ganglion, innervating the iris and pupillary ciliary muscles. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve synapses in the otic ganglion, and what does it innervate?","choices":["CN IX; innervates the parotid glands","CN III; innervates the iris","CN X; innervates the parotid glands","CN VII; innervates the parotid glands"],"answer":0,"exp":"CN IX synapses in the otic ganglion, innervating the parotid glands. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve synapses in terminal ganglia of the heart, lungs, GI tract, and other abdominal organs?","choices":["CN VII (facial)","CN IX (9th)","CN X (vagus)","CN III (3rd)"],"answer":2,"exp":"CN X (the vagus nerve) synapses in the terminal ganglia of the heart, lungs, GI tract, and other abdominal organs. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What nerves carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that synapse outside the cranial vault, to the pelvic viscera?","choices":["CN III, VII, IX, and X only","The greater splanchnic nerve","The vagus nerve exclusively","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S1-S4)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S1-S4) synapse in the terminal ganglia of the distal colon/rectum and anus, and the pelvic viscera (genitourinary tract), outside the cranial vault. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What are the parasympathetic nerves innervating the GI tract, and what are their general effects?","choices":["Splanchnic and hypogastric nerves; decrease GI peristalsis and secretion, constricting sphincters","Vagus and pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4); increase GI peristalsis/secretion and relax GI sphincters","Pelvic splanchnic nerves only; constrict GI sphincters and decrease overall intestinal motility","Vagus nerve only; decreases GI peristalsis and secretion throughout the entire digestive tract"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parasympathetic GI innervation comes from the vagus nerve and pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4), with afferent sensory nerves providing conscious sensation (hunger, distention, nausea) and effects including increased GI peristalsis/secretion and relaxation of GI sphincters. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What are the sympathetic prevertebral ganglia and nerves innervating the GI tract, and what are their general effects?","choices":["Terminal ganglia located entirely within the gut wall itself; produce no measurable effect on GI sphincter tone whatsoever, per this material","Celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric ganglia; splanchnic/hypogastric/colonic nerves; decrease GI peristalsis/secretion and constrict GI sphincters","Ciliary and otic ganglia; vagus nerve and pelvic splanchnic nerves; increase overall GI peristalsis and relax the sphincters throughout","Only the celiac ganglion, acting entirely alone without other ganglia; increases overall GI secretion and peristalsis rate significantly throughout"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic GI innervation involves the celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric prevertebral ganglia, with splanchnic, hypogastric, and colonic nerves, producing decreased GI peristalsis/secretion and constriction of GI sphincters. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is the submucosal plexus, and what does it regulate?","choices":["Located entirely outside the GI tract wall, floating freely within the peritoneal cavity","Located within the muscularis externa layer; coordinates smooth muscle contractions for peristalsis","Located in the submucosa; regulates fluid and electrolyte movement across the intestinal mucosa","Located within the circular muscle layer specifically; has no known regulatory function at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"The submucosal plexus is located in the submucosa and regulates fluid and electrolyte movement across the intestinal mucosa. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What is the myenteric plexus, and what does it coordinate?","choices":["Located in the muscularis externa between the longitudinal and circular smooth muscle layers; coordinates smooth muscle contractions involved in peristalsis","Located entirely outside the GI tract wall, floating freely within the surrounding mesenteric tissue and vasculature; has no known motor function whatsoever","Located within the submucosal layer specifically, just beneath the mucosa; regulates fluid and electrolyte movement across the entire intestinal wall","Located directly within the mucosal epithelium itself; regulates only enzyme secretion into the intestinal lumen, and nothing beyond that"],"answer":0,"exp":"The myenteric plexus is located in the muscularis externa, between the longitudinal and circular layers of smooth muscle in the GI tract wall, and coordinates smooth muscle contractions involved in peristalsis. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Where is the deep muscular plexus located within the GI tract wall?","choices":["Within the circular muscle layer","Within the mucosal epithelium","Outside the GI tract entirely","Within the submucosal layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"The deep muscular plexus is located within the circular muscle layer of the GI tract wall. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"How do sympathetic and parasympathetic efferent fibers generally interact with the enteric nervous system, per the material?","choices":["Only sympathetic fibers interact directly with the enteric nervous system in any meaningful way at all; parasympathetic fibers never synapse with it under any circumstance whatsoever, per this material","Only parasympathetic fibers interact directly with the enteric nervous system in any meaningful way at all; sympathetic fibers never synapse with it under any circumstance whatsoever, per this material","They bypass the enteric nervous system entirely in every single case, innervating the smooth muscle and mucosal tissues directly and exclusively themselves, with absolutely no intermediary neurons involved at all","They innervate the enteric nervous system itself (not directly the smooth muscle or mucosal tissues), with the exception of direct sympathetic innervation of submucosal arteries and sphincteric smooth muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sympathetic and parasympathetic efferent fibers innervate the enteric nervous system (not the smooth muscle or mucosal tissues of the GI tract directly), with the exception of direct sympathetic innervation of submucosal arteries and sphincteric smooth muscle. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"}]};
